{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfuigcjGH891cy3FjGGo3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Mostly no. A normal `wrangler deploy` swaps every Durable Object over to the new code right away. Each object gets restarted, which drops its in-memory state and disconnects its WebSockets. There's no setting that lets an object finish its current life on the old code.\n\nThe closest option is **gradual deployments**. Instead of `wrangler deploy`, run `wrangler versions upload` and then `wrangler versions deploy`, and split traffic between the old and new versions (for example 90/10). Each object ID is pinned to one version by a hash, so objects still assigned to the old version keep running it. You then move objects over in stages by raising the new version's percentage. Each object still restarts when it switches, but you choose when that happens and how many switch at once.\n\nYou should still make your objects handle restarts cleanly: store important state with the storage API, and have clients reconnect their WebSockets automatically. I answered this from memory, so check Cloudflare's current Durable Objects and gradual deployments docs for any newer options."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5824,"cache_read_input_tokens":15764,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5824},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"266f5e13-6fd6-4c02-871d-5f37ec1390db","uuid":"ce91e440-8b15-4a09-b103-ae132b8d998b","timestamp":"2026-10-11T03:13:05.927Z","request_id":"req_011CfuigcUdCA254FBFR9Kfb"}
